# Line endings.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows, where core.autocrlf=true is the norm and
# every text file is checked out with CRLF. That is harmless for .py/.ts/.md —
# and fatal for a shell script: bash reads the trailing \r as part of the
# command and fails with the famously unhelpful "\r: command not found", or
# "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r: no such file or directory". So shell scripts are
# pinned to LF no matter what the cloner's autocrlf says.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The mirror image: PowerShell and the .cmd wrapper are Windows-side tools and
# stay CRLF, which is what a Windows editor and cmd.exe expect.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Everything else: normalise to LF in the repository, let git decide locally.
* text=auto
